{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3e3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b131-d4f6-7b82-a7a6-5363cf3ac7fb"},"sequence":76,"recorded_at":1780531400000147,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd"},"call_id":"call_01a0b131f30e745284f15d9c6efc722c","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1geqhj/TASK-260830-1geqhj_producer.md`.\n1|Own TASK-260830-1geqhj (implement-ax-pane-enforcement-wrapper), first leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe (ax-pane-and-terminal-instance-binding, EPIC M1; siblings 2056mm and kkh1an follow). Implement, as production code over the landed owners, the validation core of the `ax pane SESSION_ID` wrapper that every managed pane runs (pinned Section 4.C wrapper rule: validate configuration, load the logical session, synchronize known lease records when possible, compare the local fencing token before launching a provider, park safely when ownership is remote, ambiguous or unverified; the after-restore sequence 1-5: read the latest locally known lease, attempt a bounded mesh lease refresh, resume locally only with the winning lease AND a valid required materialization, offer remote attach/takeover when another host owns the session interactively, otherwise enter parked without launching the provider; terminal creation and the wrapper's first child start idempotent on (session_id, bootstrap_operation_id) with the pair durably bound before child creation, identical retry reattaching to the one recorded wrapper/child, changed operation in the bootstrap window = idempotency_mismatch, status plus the binding proving absence or identifying the one child after a lost result; never persist or replicate a PID; no credential-dependent tmux server creation from a background caller — capability_unavailable with typed realm/readiness details, never direct server creation; cached sentinel/managername never authorizes resume), Section 4.B (Terminal Backend Manifest/Probe identity through the landed internal/terminalbackend registry and contract), Section 4.D (closed capabilities and evidence: only the exact admitted capability rows authorize an action; no capability map invented), Section 5.2 Terminal Events (the events the wrapper authors: session.parked with its reason vocabulary, terminal binding/evidence events as the schema defines them — author them through the landed sessrepo append path under the current lease), Section 7.A (Provider Protocol 3.0.0 Terminal Instance binding descriptor: terminal_binding_id, terminal_instance_id UUIDv7 never a PID/handle, backend id/version/protocol/generation, interactive, columns/rows — the wrapper supplies it and the provider must reject a mismatched binding; the LeaseToken is passed separately) and Section 13.1 Direct-session launch steps 3-4 (create the durable terminal entry with the bootstrap operation ID, then provider launch with its validated argv/env plan). The wrapper's checks must be composed from the landed gates rather than re-implemented: owner epoch/fencing through internal/fencing (AuthorizeActivation/Input/Restore, the sealed LeaseToken projection, park vocabulary), materialization validity through internal/matjournal + internal/sessckpt admission, profile through internal/sessprofile (effective persisted profile + Section 7.7 mapping refusals, profile_mapping_unavailable), provider identity through internal/provhost (CreateIdentity/Verify*, resume tuple gate, resumesmoke evidence as a precondition input), resume authorization through the fencing gates plus the checkpoint admission. Deliver a library entry (for example internal/axpane) with a pure decision core (inputs: config, session record + chain, local lease view, materialization/journal state, profile, provider identity, terminal backend probe, realm readiness; output: exactly one of launch|reattach|attach_remote|takeover_offer|parked(reason)|refused(class)) plus the durable bootstrap binding (session_id, bootstrap_operation_id) under the landed no-replace + fsync discipline with crash hooks and a real-kill test; no `ax` CLI, no tmux/ConPTY process control, no provider process — those are modeled callers and later leaves (state the bound). Tests: every decision outcome driven with positives and the full refusal/park table (lower/losing epoch, foreign session, expired grant, absent/ambiguous/remote ownership, invalid materialization, unmapped profile, unresolved provider identity or realm, backend probe mismatch, background caller without an attested server, changed bootstrap operation in-window, lost-result reattach), the after-restore sequence as a scenario table, idempotency of the bootstrap binding, crash at the binding seam, narrowing mutants per gate composition (each composition check dropped or reordered must be caught), and a conformance matrix over 4.B/4.C/4.D/5.2/7.A/13.1 clauses.\n2|COMMON CONTRACT (first leaf of a Story on trunk 7efe385 or later). Normative authority: the pinned internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md headings named below; the v0.7.0 adoption (STORY-260916-3uqbwq) is landing during your run and is byte-identical in these sections — if it has landed when you refresh, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers. Reuse the landed owners (canonicaljson closed shapes, scalar, sessrepo/sessstate/sessquery, sessckpt, matjournal, crashgate, fencing, sessprofile, provhost/resumesmoke, hosttrust/peeridentity/sshtransport, rpcwire/hostchannel, sessadapter, terminalbackend, localstore, secprim/secconftest) — never fork a second model; extend or adopt. Traceability: do NOT edit internal/traceability (ownership registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) in this first leaf — the Story's FINAL leaf carries the registry bindings and re-pin once the v0.7.0 registry is on trunk; document your clause coverage in a package-local TRACEABILITY.md and the conformance matrix instead. Evidence contract (repository DoD): every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write (landed no-replace + fsync + hooks + real-kill pattern) or an explicit purity bound; conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test or a stated bound; README package section with test commands and NO capability/CLI claim (no ax command exists); LOGBOOK entry newest-first. Managed Story worktree contract: .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-ptxkqe forked from current trunk; leave the candidate UNCOMMITTED; never commit on the Story branch, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept; if origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-1geqhj` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\"checkpoint_oid\": <REBASE_HEAD original checkpoint>, \"path\": \"LOGBOOK.md\", \"sha256\": <hex of the full replacement>, \"content\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that appears in your git status and restore trunk content by hand (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json == HEAD); before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured 26-command validation suite once before handoff (command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`); hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-1geqhj_results.md, TASK-260830-1geqhj_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-1geqhj_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1geqhj --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3e5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b131-d4f6-7b82-a7a6-5363cf3ac7fb"},"sequence":77,"recorded_at":1780531400000149,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd"},"call_id":"call_01a0b131f4bf72bfac27445f8a1a2ac0","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1geqhj/TASK-260830-1geqhj_rework-rev3.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES EVERY EARLIER PRECONDITION ON TASK-260830-1geqhj, including TASK-260830-1geqhj_producer.md and TASK-260830-1geqhj_rework-rev2.md. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-1geqhj (implement-ax-pane-enforcement-wrapper, first leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe). Change Request revision 2 was reviewed by RUN-260917-368f1f (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: one P1 class, two P2, eight P3. Read TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-verdict-rev2.md and TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-evidence-rev2.tar.gz before touching code. ELEVEN of the twelve rev1 findings were graded FIXED on the reviewer's own executed evidence, including every rev1 P1 vector and the three previously surviving mutants (RM1, RM2, RM4 all die twice now). Do not rewrite any of that. The remaining P1 is one root cause that several rev1 fixes were built on, and it is partly MY fault as the orchestrator: the rev2 brief told you to close the bootstrap window \"using the winner's own HasCheckpoint/epoch already present in Observation\", and that hint is only correct for epoch >= 2. You followed it faithfully; the fix below replaces it with the right fact.\n3|P1-1 — the window, the checkpoint admission and the journal binding are keyed on the LEASE RECORD's checkpoint, which is null for every epoch-1 owner forever. `decide.go:507` (bootstrapWindowClosed), `decide.go:599` (admitCheckpoint) and `decide.go:571` (checkMaterialization) all read `Observation.Winner.HasCheckpoint`/`Winner.Checkpoint`. Lease records are immutable content-addressed blobs: `sessrepo.CreateLease` mints the epoch-1 lease with a null checkpoint and only `CompareAndSwapLease` (a takeover, epoch >= 2) mints one carrying a checkpoint (Section 5.3: \"Null only for epoch-1 create; otherwise the validated materialized handoff base\"). Nothing ever writes a checkpoint onto the epoch-1 lease however many checkpoints the session publishes. Executed consequences: (a) the ordinary same-host story — created, idle, stopped with checkpoint C, landed engine reporting `state=stopped HasCheckpoint=true newest=C` while the winning lease says `epoch=1 HasCheckpoint=false` — restores with a new bootstrap operation and is REFUSED `idempotency_mismatch` \"inside the bootstrap window\": the rev1 P1-3 symptom, alive for every session that never changed hands; (b) in that same state a second attested checkpoint C2 that the chain never published is ADMITTED and launches — \"admit exactly the winning lease's checkpoint\" admits anything when the lease carries none; (c) a committed journal sourced from a digest no lease and no event names launches, through `Run`, with a bootstrap binding installed. Required fix: derive the newest checkpoint through the landed chain fold — `sessstate.Reduce` -> `Projection.HasCheckpoint` / `Newest.ID` from `checkpoint.created`, checkpointed `session.stopped` and `session.resumed` (Section 5.7 `newest_checkpoint_id`, Section 14.4) — which this package ALREADY composes in `canAuthorParked` and never consults in these three places. Close the window on that fact (epoch >= 2 implies it); admit exactly that checkpoint when a checkpoint is required; bind a required journal's `SourceCheckpointID` to it; and park `restore_policy` when it is null and a checkpoint or materialization is required (the only launch-class path left in that state is the bootstrap retry without a checkpoint). A successor lease's `Checkpoint` is the handoff base and must AGREE with the fold — assert that, do not choose one and ignore the other. Then re-pin the three committed positives that currently pin the broken state as a launch (`TestDecideTable/launch_restore_committed_checkpoint`, `TestDecideAfterRestoreSequence/step3_resume_winning_lease_valid_materialization`, `TestRunRestoreCommitted`) with the chain actually carrying the checkpoint, add the epoch-1-owner negatives for all three gates at both `Decide` and `Run`, and ship a narrowing mutant on each of the three conditionals.\n4|P2-1 — post-window supersession destroys the prior pair's receipt. `Supersede` (binding.go:221) renames the new receipt over the single `binding.json`, so the superseded `(session_id, bootstrap_operation_id)` receipt is gone: post-window op2 launches, op3 launches, then an IDENTICAL RETRY OF op2 launches a third child instead of replaying op2's receipt, and `Status` afterwards identifies only the last child. Section 4.1 requires an identical retry to return or reattach to the one recorded wrapper/child, and Section 4.C keys that receipt by `session_id + \"/\" + bootstrap_operation_id`. Keep the superseded receipts (per-operation files under the session directory, or an append-only receipt set) so an identical retry of ANY recorded pair reattaches to its own child, and make the post-window install no-replace per pair — two `Supersede` calls with different operations currently both succeed because the rename carries no no-replace guard.\n5|P2-2 — `Run` silently derives the launch profile from the session head when no checkpoint store is bound. run.go:190 loads the winner's closure only `if ... && stores.Ckpt != nil`; otherwise it falls back to `sessprofile.Derive` over the head, so a winner carrying a checkpoint plus an authoritative head-only `profile.changed` launches `yolo` with the head pair when `stores.Ckpt == nil`, and `standard`/null when the store is bound. An absent store is UNKNOWN, not \"no closure\": mirror the `CheckpointRequired` guard at run.go:169 and fail the run when the winner carries a checkpoint and no checkpoint store is bound.\n6|P3 (all eight, each named in the verdict with its probe): P3-1 pin the losing-epoch arm of the `Emit` mutation gate with a chain that FOLDS, so the refusal comes from `AuthorizeMutation` and not from the fold pre-check (mutant RV7 survives today); P3-2 pin create-from-stopped through `Run` (RV11 survived twice); P3-3 the coverage denominator was restated, not re-derived — the TRACEABILITY acceptance table has 43 rows while results, TRACEABILITY and the matrix all say \"41 of 41\"; the reviewer measures 39 of 43 driven faithfully, so re-derive the ratio yourself and publish the honest number with the gaps named; P3-4 `checkRealm` composes the Section 4.C credential conditional only for background callers — a foreground credential-requiring caller bypasses it; P3-5 `checkRealmBinding` returns on the first `credential_capable_execution_realm` object so the outcome depends on input order; P3-6 an expired or pre-reboot realm row refuses as `terminal_backend_manifest_probe_mismatch`/`terminal_backend_stale_generation` instead of the Section 4.2 `capability_unavailable` with typed realm/readiness details; P3-7 test hygiene (`TestRunTakeoverOfferEmitsParked` now asserts a non-interactive park with no emission — the name is stale; `TestRunPostWindowSupersedes` drops the materialization requirement instead of sourcing the journal); P3-8 the rev1 LOGBOOK entry's F1 claim is now false and the rev2 entry only reverses it implicitly — say so explicitly.\n7|Procedure: TRUNK HAS MOVED. origin/main is now 2fc6d5074058ab7970c87982deac50b3e3f5fd91 and carries the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52): internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json. Your candidate sits on base 888ae3d. Run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-1geqhj` FIRST, then audit every path in `git diff --name-only 888ae3d 2fc6d50` that also appears in your `git status` — README.md and LOGBOOK.md both moved on trunk with that landing, so your edits must be purely additive on top of the NEW content and must never restore the old bytes; task-board.config.json must end byte-identical to the new base. You are a FIRST leaf: do NOT touch internal/traceability (the final leaf TASK-260830-2056mm carries the registry bindings and the pin). Once refreshed, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept. Re-run the full configured 27-command validation suite once (command 5 `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — expected, do not shorten it), update TASK-260830-1geqhj_results.md with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path), update the conformance matrix and TASK-260830-1geqhj_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive, one raw log per plant with its subprocess exit, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__), reset and re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1geqhj --role developer` so revision 3 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in.\n8|PARTIAL-ATTEMPT RECOVERY: this task has already lost runs to a provider \"model stream idle timeout\" (the runtime cancels the run and spawns a successor that re-reads this brief from the start). The Story worktree is NOT reset when that happens — it still carries whatever the dead attempt wrote. Before anything else run `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree status --short` and `git diff`, write into your results exactly what was already present when you started, and verify every pre-existing change against this brief instead of assuming it is correct.\n9|Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 3.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3e7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b131-d4f6-7b82-a7a6-5363cf3ac7fb"},"sequence":78,"recorded_at":1780531400000151,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"2237b1e3-856b-4a22-9f46-61adba667efd"},"call_id":"call_01a0b131f64b726d872d88a903e833a7","text":"<read-skill-result name=\"go-testing-tools\" status=\"ok\">\n<metadata>\npath: /Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nlocator: file:///Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nsource: user\nbytes_returned: 15216\nbytes_total: 15216\ntruncated: false\n</metadata>\n\n<skill-body id=\"go-testing-tools\">\n---\nname: go-testing-tools\ndescription: &gt;\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\ntriggers:\n  - go test\n  - bubbletea test\n  - tuitestkit\n  - go test helpers\n  - reducer test\n  - snapshot test go\n  - golden file go\n  - тесты го\n  - го тест хелперы\n  - тесты баблти\n  - тест утилиты го\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n---\n\n# Go Testing Tools\n\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **Go 1.21+**\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n\nInstall the library in your project:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n\n```go\npackage myscreen_test\n\nimport (\n    &quot;testing&quot;\n\n    &quot;github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit&quot;\n)\n\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n\n    // Press down twice\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;), tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;))\n\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, &quot;third item&quot;)\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, &quot;ERROR&quot;)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing Pyramid\n\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 &gt; Level 2 &gt; Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n\n---\n\n## Library API Reference\n\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n\n```go\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n// Supports: &quot;enter&quot;, &quot;tab&quot;, &quot;esc&quot;, &quot;space&quot;, &quot;backspace&quot;, &quot;up&quot;, &quot;down&quot;,\n// &quot;left&quot;, &quot;right&quot;, &quot;home&quot;, &quot;end&quot;, &quot;pgup&quot;, &quot;pgdown&quot;, &quot;delete&quot;, &quot;insert&quot;,\n// &quot;f1&quot;-&quot;f20&quot;, &quot;ctrl+c&quot;, &quot;ctrl+a&quot;...&quot;ctrl+z&quot;, &quot;alt+h&quot;, &quot;alt+enter&quot;,\n// single runes: &quot;a&quot;, &quot;/&quot;, &quot;1&quot;, etc.\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n```\n\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n\n```go\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n// Init() is NOT called.\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n```\n\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n\n```go\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;r&quot;))\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n```\n\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n\n```go\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n// S = state type, A = action type.\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Action  A\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name   string\n    Action A\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n```\n\n**Invariant checking:**\n\n```go\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\n    Name  string\n    Check func(s S) error\n}\n\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```go\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n        Name: &quot;cursor in bounds&quot;,\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n            if s.Cursor &lt; 0 || s.Cursor &gt;= len(s.Items) {\n                return fmt.Errorf(&quot;cursor %d out of [0, %d)&quot;, s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n            }\n            return nil\n        },\n    },\n)\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n```\n\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n\n```go\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\ntype MockCall struct {\n    Method string\n    Args   []any\n}\n\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n\n// Assertion helpers\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n```\n\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n\n```go\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n}\n\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n    return &amp;TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;Execute&quot;, cmd, args)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;Execute:&quot; + cmd)\n}\n```\n\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n\n```go\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\n\n// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\n\nfunc ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\nfunc ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\n\n// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\n\nfunc ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc LinesFromStr(view string) []string\nfunc MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\n```\n\n### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\n\nGolden file comparison for visual regression testing.\n\n```go\n// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\n// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\nvar UpdateSnapshots bool\n\n// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\nfunc SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n```\n\nGolden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/&lt;name&gt;.golden` relative to the test file.\n\n```bash\n# Create or update golden files\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\n\n# Update for a single package\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\n```\n\nMismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\n\n---\n\n## Closed-Loop Workflow\n\nThe agent development cycle for writing tests:\n\n```\n1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\n2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\n3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\n4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\n5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\n6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\n7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\n8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\n9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\n```\n\n### Step Details\n\n**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\n- What state does it manage?\n- What messages does it handle in Update?\n- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\n- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\n- What does View() render?\n\n**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\n- Pure state logic -&gt; Level 1 (reducer tests)\n- Key bindings, view rendering -&gt; Level 2 (component tests)\n- Data loading, command execution -&gt; Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\n- Visual appearance -&gt; Level 4 (snapshot tests)\n\n**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\n1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\n2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\n3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\n4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\n\n**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\n- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\n- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\n- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\n- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\n\n**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\n\n**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\n\n**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\n- Type assertion panic -&gt; `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\n- View assertion failure -&gt; check ANSI stripping, check exact text\n- Snapshot mismatch -&gt; run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\n- Mock assertion failure -&gt; check response key matches (`&quot;Execute:list&quot;` not `&quot;Execute&quot;`)\n\n---\n\n## Templates\n\nScaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\n\n| Template | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\n| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\n| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\n| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\n| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\n| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\n\nCopy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\nIn-depth documentation in `references/`:\n\n| Document | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\n| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\n| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\n\n---\n\n## Setup\n\nRun the setup script to create symlinks:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nThis links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\n\nTo remove:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/deinit.sh\n```\n\n</skill-body>\n</read-skill-result>","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_skill","outcome":"success"}}}
